function fs_new_content_guides_4b() {
    return [
    [
        'slug'      => 'tax-loss-harvesting',
        'title'     => 'Tax Loss Harvesting: How It Works, Rules and Examples',
        'keyword'   => 'tax loss harvesting',
        'desc'      => 'Tax loss harvesting explained: how selling losers cuts your tax bill, the wash-sale rule, the $3,000 limit, 2026 capital gains brackets and an example.',
        'excerpt'   => 'A plain-English guide to tax loss harvesting, with the IRS rules, the wash-sale trap, 2026 capital gains brackets and a worked example.',
        'publish_at' => '2026-10-27 09:00',
        'answer'    => 'Tax loss harvesting means selling an investment in a taxable account for less than you paid so the loss offsets capital gains. Up to $3,000 of net loss a year ($1,500 if married filing separately) can also offset ordinary income, and the rest carries forward. Avoid buying substantially identical securities 30 days before or after the sale.',
        'takeaways' => [
            'Harvested losses offset capital gains first, then up to $3,000 of ordinary income a year.',
            'Net losses above the $3,000 limit carry forward to later tax years.',
            'The wash-sale rule blocks the loss if you buy substantially identical securities within 30 days, including in an IRA.',
            'The sale must have a trade date of December 31, 2026 or earlier to count for 2026.',
        ],
        'content'   => '<p class="lead"><strong>Tax loss harvesting</strong> is the practice of selling an investment in a taxable brokerage account for less than you paid, so the realized loss reduces the tax you owe on other gains. It does not make a losing investment profitable, but it can turn a paper loss into a real tax benefit. This guide explains what tax loss harvesting is, how it works under IRS rules, the wash-sale trap, the $3,000 annual limit and a worked example with real numbers. To estimate the tax on your own gains, use the <a href="{tool:capital-gains-tax-calculator}">capital gains tax calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>What is tax loss harvesting?</h2><p>Tax-loss harvesting is a strategy, not a special tax provision. The IRS taxes capital gains when you sell an asset for more than its cost basis and lets you deduct capital losses when you sell for less. Harvesting simply means choosing to realize losses on purpose, usually near the end of the year, so they offset gains you have already realized or expect to realize.</p><p>Most people who harvest losses want to stay invested, so they sell the losing position and buy something similar, but not "substantially identical", to keep their market exposure. That last detail is where the rules matter most.</p><p>Harvesting only applies to taxable accounts. Gains and losses inside a traditional IRA, Roth IRA or 401(k) are not reported each year, so selling at a loss there produces no deduction.</p>'
            . '<h2>Latest update: what changed for 2026 and 2027</h2><ul><li><strong>2026 brackets are set.</strong> The 2026 capital gains thresholds below come from IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32, published in Internal Revenue Bulletin 2025-45 on November 3, 2025. The 2025 tax law (the One Big Beautiful Bill Act) kept the 0%, 15% and 20% rate structure, and the wash-sale rule and the $3,000 loss limit are unchanged.</li><li><strong>2027 figures are not out yet.</strong> As of October 24, 2026, the IRS newsroom had not released the tax year 2027 inflation adjustments, which will update the capital gains thresholds. The IRS usually publishes them in the fall; this guide will be updated when they appear.</li><li><strong>The $3,000 limit does not rise with inflation.</strong> It is not among the items in the IRS’s annual inflation adjustments, so it stays at $3,000 ($1,500 married filing separately).</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>How does tax loss harvesting work?</h2><p>The mechanics follow the order the IRS uses on Schedule D. According to <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Publication 550</a>, gains and losses are first grouped by holding period, then combined:</p><ol><li><strong>Sort by holding period.</strong> If you held an asset for more than one year, the gain or loss is long-term. One year or less is short-term (<a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc409" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Topic 409</a>).</li><li><strong>Net within each group.</strong> Short-term losses offset short-term gains; long-term losses offset long-term gains.</li><li><strong>Net across groups.</strong> A net loss in one group offsets a net gain in the other.</li><li><strong>Deduct up to $3,000.</strong> If you still have a net loss, you can deduct the lesser of $3,000 ($1,500 if married filing separately) or your total net loss against ordinary income such as wages.</li><li><strong>Carry the rest forward.</strong> Any loss above that limit carries forward to later years. When figuring the carryover, the IRS has you use short-term losses first.</li></ol><p>The order matters because short-term gains are taxed at ordinary income rates, while long-term gains usually get the lower 0%, 15% or 20% rates. A loss that wipes out a short-term gain is generally worth more than one that offsets a long-term gain.</p>'
            . '<h2>Tax loss harvesting rules: the wash-sale rule</h2><p>The wash-sale rule is the main limit on harvesting. IRS Publication 550 says a wash sale occurs when you sell or trade stock or securities at a loss and, within 30 days before or after the sale, you:</p><ul><li>buy substantially identical stock or securities,</li><li>acquire substantially identical stock or securities in a fully taxable trade,</li><li>acquire a contract or option to buy substantially identical stock or securities, or</li><li>acquire substantially identical stock for your IRA or Roth IRA.</li></ul><p>The window covers 61 days in total: 30 days before the sale, the sale date and 30 days after. A purchase by your spouse or by a corporation you control also counts. The IRA rule comes from <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rr-08-05.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Revenue Ruling 2008-5</a>, which held that a loss is disallowed when an IRA or Roth IRA buys substantially identical stock within the window, and that the IRA’s basis is not increased. In that case the loss is effectively gone for good, not just postponed.</p><h3>What happens to a disallowed loss?</h3><p>In an ordinary wash sale in a taxable account, the loss is not lost. Publication 550 says you add the disallowed loss to the cost of the new shares, and the holding period of the old shares carries over. The deduction is postponed until you sell the replacement shares.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> you buy 200 shares for $10,000 and sell them for $7,600, a $2,400 loss. Twenty days later you buy 200 shares of the same stock for $7,800. The $2,400 loss is disallowed, and your basis in the new shares becomes $7,800 + $2,400 = $10,200.</p><h3>What counts as substantially identical?</h3><p>The IRS says the answer depends on all the facts and circumstances. Shares of one company are ordinarily not substantially identical to shares of a different company. The IRS has not published a bright-line test for mutual funds and exchange-traded funds, so investors who swap one index fund for another that tracks a different index are relying on a judgment, not a written safe harbor. Buying the same fund, or shares of the same company, inside the window is clearly a wash sale.</p><h3>Watch automatic purchases</h3><p>Dividend reinvestment, automatic monthly investments and purchases in a spouse’s account or your IRA can all fall inside the 30-day window. Pause or redirect them before you harvest a loss. Brokers report wash sales they can see on Form 1099-B, but the IRS notes you cannot deduct a wash-sale loss even if it is not reported to you.</p>'
            . '<h2>2026 long-term capital gains tax brackets</h2><p>The value of a harvested loss depends on the rate it saves. For tax year 2026, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-32.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32</a> sets these taxable income thresholds for the long-term capital gains rates:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Filing status (2026)</th><th>0% rate up to</th><th>15% rate up to</th><th>20% rate above</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Single</td><td>$49,450</td><td>$545,500</td><td>$545,500</td></tr><tr><td>Married filing jointly / surviving spouse</td><td>$98,900</td><td>$613,700</td><td>$613,700</td></tr><tr><td>Married filing separately</td><td>$49,450</td><td>$306,850</td><td>$306,850</td></tr><tr><td>Head of household</td><td>$66,200</td><td>$579,600</td><td>$579,600</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Higher earners may also owe the 3.8% net investment income tax. According to <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc559" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Topic 559</a>, it applies when modified adjusted gross income exceeds $200,000 for single filers and heads of household, $250,000 for joint filers and $125,000 for married filing separately. If your income falls in the 0% band, harvesting losses against long-term gains may save nothing; some investors in that band deliberately realize gains instead.</p>'
            . '<h2>Tax loss harvesting explained with a worked example</h2><p>Take a single filer with a 24% marginal tax rate on ordinary income and a 15% rate on long-term gains. During 2026 she has already realized:</p><ul><li>a $4,000 short-term gain from a stock held eight months, and</li><li>a $10,000 long-term gain from a fund held five years.</li></ul><p>She also holds another fund, owned for three years, that is worth $18,000 less than she paid. In December she sells it, realizing an $18,000 long-term loss, and buys a fund that tracks a different index.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Short-term</th><th>Long-term</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gains realized</td><td>+$4,000</td><td>+$10,000</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Harvested loss</td><td>$0</td><td>&minus;$18,000</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Net within each group</td><td>+$4,000</td><td>&minus;$8,000</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Net across groups</td><td colspan="2">&minus;$8,000 + $4,000</td><td>&minus;$4,000 net loss</td></tr><tr><td>Deduct against ordinary income</td><td colspan="2"></td><td>$3,000</td></tr><tr><td>Carry forward to 2027</td><td colspan="2"></td><td>$1,000 (long-term)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Estimated 2026 tax saved: $4,000 of short-term gain no longer taxed at 24% ($960), $10,000 of long-term gain no longer taxed at 15% ($1,500) and a $3,000 deduction against ordinary income at 24% ($720), for a total of <strong>$3,180</strong>, plus a $1,000 loss to use next year.</p><p>Two caveats keep this honest. First, the replacement fund now has a lower cost basis, so more of its future growth will be taxable when sold; harvesting often defers tax rather than erasing it. Second, the saving only exists if she would otherwise have paid tax on those gains. Run your own numbers with the <a href="{tool:capital-gains-tax-calculator}">capital gains tax calculator</a> and the <a href="{tool:income-tax-calculator}">income tax calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Direct indexing tax loss harvesting</h2><p>Direct indexing means owning the individual stocks in an index directly, rather than one fund that holds them. Because each stock is a separate position, some can be sold at a loss even in a year when the index as a whole is up. That creates more harvesting opportunities than a single index fund, where you can only harvest when the whole fund is below your cost.</p><p>The trade-offs are cost, complexity and tracking. Direct indexing accounts often have higher minimums and fees than a plain index fund, they generate many more transactions to report, and the harvested positions must be replaced with different stocks for at least 31 days, so the portfolio can drift from the index. Compare those costs with the likely tax saving, which shrinks over time as the remaining positions accumulate gains. Our <a href="/index-fund-investing-beginners-guide-2026/">index fund investing guide</a> explains the simpler alternative.</p>'
            . '<h2>Do robo-advisors and brokerages harvest losses automatically?</h2><p>Many automated investment services and some brokerages offer automatic tax loss harvesting, often as an optional feature. The SEC’s <a href="https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-45" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Investor Bulletin on robo-advisers</a> notes that tax loss harvesting may have tax implications and that its value depends on your particular tax situation. If you search for a specific firm’s service, read that firm’s own disclosure for how it chooses replacement securities and whether it coordinates with accounts it does not manage. An automated program cannot see purchases in your spouse’s account or your IRA at another firm, and those can still create wash sales.</p>'
            . '<h2>When is tax harvesting loss less useful?</h2><ul><li><strong>Your income is in the 0% long-term capital gains band.</strong> Offsetting gains that would be taxed at 0% saves nothing.</li><li><strong>The loss is small.</strong> Trading costs, bid-ask spreads and time can outweigh a modest saving.</li><li><strong>You plan to hold until death or donate the shares.</strong> Heirs may receive a stepped-up basis, and donating appreciated shares to charity can avoid the gain, so lowering your basis now may cost more later.</li><li><strong>You would sell an investment you still want.</strong> Never let the tax tail wag the investment dog; the replacement should fit your plan on its own merits. A quick check with the <a href="{tool:portfolio-analyzer}">portfolio analyzer</a> helps confirm your mix after a swap.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>What is the year-end deadline?</h2><p>For securities traded on an established market, Publication 550 says your holding period ends on the trade date you sold them, and a sale on December 31 is reported for that year even if it settles in January. So a loss must have a <strong>trade date of December 31, 2026 or earlier</strong> to count on your 2026 return. Remember that the wash-sale window also looks 30 days back, so purchases of the same security in early December can affect a late-December sale. Harvesting is one item on our <a href="/year-end-tax-planning-checklist/">year-end tax planning checklist</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>How to report harvested losses</h2><p>Sales are reported on Form 8949 and summarized on Schedule D. Your broker sends Form 1099-B showing proceeds, cost basis and any wash-sale adjustment it identified. If you have a wash sale the broker did not report, the IRS instructions say to enter code "W" in column (f) and the disallowed amount in column (g) of Form 8949. Keep records of carryovers; the Capital Loss Carryover Worksheet in Publication 550 shows how to figure them.</p>'
            . '<h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/year-end-tax-planning-checklist/">Year-end tax planning checklist for 2026</a></li><li><a href="/index-fund-investing-beginners-guide-2026/">Index fund investing for beginners</a></li><li><a href="/retirement-planning-how-much-need-2026/">How much you need to retire</a></li><li><a href="{tool:dividend-calculator}">Dividend calculator</a> and <a href="{tool:compound-interest-calculator}">compound interest calculator</a></li></ul>'
            . '<p><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Publication 550</a> (wash sales, netting, capital loss limit, trade date); <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rr-08-05.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Revenue Ruling 2008-5</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-32.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32</a> (2026 capital gains thresholds); <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc409" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Topic 409</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc559" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Topic 559</a>; <a href="https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-45" rel="noopener" target="_blank">SEC Investor Bulletin: Robo-Advisers</a>. Figures checked October 24, 2026.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal tax or financial advice.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'What is tax loss harvesting?', 'Tax loss harvesting is selling an investment in a taxable account for less than you paid so the realized loss offsets capital gains. Any net loss left over can reduce ordinary income by up to $3,000 a year, or $1,500 if married filing separately, and the remainder carries forward to future years.' ],
            [ 'What is tax-loss harvesting worth in a typical year?', 'It depends on your gains and tax rates. In our example, an $18,000 harvested loss saved about $3,180 in 2026 for a single filer in the 24% bracket with $14,000 of gains. If your gains would be taxed at 0%, the saving can be close to nothing.' ],
            [ 'What are the tax loss harvesting rules for wash sales?', 'You cannot deduct the loss if you, your spouse, a corporation you control or your IRA buys substantially identical securities within 30 days before or after the sale. In a taxable account the disallowed loss is added to the new shares’ basis; in an IRA it is lost.' ],
            [ 'How much capital loss can I deduct per year?', 'After netting all gains and losses, you can deduct up to $3,000 of net capital loss against ordinary income, or $1,500 if married filing separately. Any loss above that carries forward to later years and is used against future gains first, then the annual allowance.' ],
            [ 'Is direct indexing tax loss harvesting better than using funds?', 'Direct indexing holds individual stocks, so some can be harvested even when the index rises, creating more opportunities. It usually costs more, needs larger balances and creates more tax reporting. Whether it is better depends on your account size, fees and how much gain you expect to offset.' ],
            [ 'Does Fidelity tax loss harvesting or another broker do it for me?', 'Several brokerages and robo-advisors offer automatic harvesting as a feature, but terms differ. Read the provider’s own disclosure. Automated programs cannot see trades in your other accounts or your spouse’s, so you remain responsible for avoiding wash sales across all of them.' ],
            [ 'What is the deadline for tax loss harvesting in 2026?', 'The sale must have a trade date on or before December 31, 2026 to count for tax year 2026, even if it settles in January 2027. Check for purchases of the same security in the 30 days before the sale, and avoid repurchasing for 30 days after.' ],
            [ 'Can I harvest losses in my IRA or 401(k)?', 'No. Gains and losses inside IRAs and 401(k) plans are not reported annually, so a loss there is not deductible. Buying substantially identical securities in an IRA within 30 days of a taxable-account loss sale also triggers a wash sale under Revenue Ruling 2008-5.' ],
        ],
    ],
    [
        'slug'       => 'should-i-refinance-my-mortgage',
        'title'      => 'Should I Refinance My Mortgage? A Break-Even Guide',
        'keyword'    => 'should i refinance my mortgage',
        'desc'       => 'Should I refinance my mortgage? Use the break-even formula, typical closing costs, a worked example, the 15-year option and when refinancing loses.',
        'excerpt'    => 'A break-even guide to refinancing a mortgage, with closing cost ranges from Freddie Mac, a worked example and when not to refinance.',
        'publish_at' => '2026-10-31 09:00',
        'answer'     => 'Refinance when the new loan saves enough each month to recover the closing costs well before you expect to sell or pay off the home. Divide total closing costs by the monthly saving to find the break-even point in months. Also compare total interest, because restarting a 30-year term can raise lifetime cost.',
        'takeaways'  => [
            'Break-even months = total closing costs divided by monthly payment savings.',
            'Freddie Mac says refinancing typically costs 3% to 6% of the loan principal.',
            'A new 30-year loan can lower the payment yet add years of interest; compare total cost too.',
            'A 15-year refinance raises the payment but can cut total interest sharply.',
        ],
        'content'    => '<p class="lead"><strong>Should I refinance my mortgage?</strong> The answer comes down to one number: how many months it takes for the lower payment to repay the cost of the new loan. If you will keep the home well past that break-even point, refinancing can save money. If not, it can cost you. This guide shows the break-even formula, typical closing costs from Freddie Mac, a worked example with real numbers, the 15-year option, cash-out refinancing and the cases where refinancing does not pay. You can run your own figures in the <a href="{tool:refinance-calculator}">refinance calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>When should I refinance my mortgage?</h2><p>Refinancing replaces your current home loan with a new one, usually to get a lower interest rate, change the term or take cash out of your equity. It tends to make sense when:</p><ul><li><strong>Rates have fallen</strong> enough below your current rate that the monthly saving recovers closing costs in a reasonable time.</li><li><strong>You plan to stay</strong> in the home longer than the break-even period.</li><li><strong>Your credit or equity has improved</strong>, which may qualify you for a better rate or let you drop mortgage insurance.</li><li><strong>You want a different loan type</strong>, for example moving from an adjustable rate to a fixed rate for predictable payments.</li><li><strong>You want to pay the loan off sooner</strong> with a shorter term.</li></ul><p>There is no universal "rate drop" rule that works for everyone. A small rate cut on a large balance can beat a bigger cut on a small balance. The break-even calculation is what tells you.</p>'
            . '<h2>Latest update: mortgage rates in October 2026</h2><p>According to Freddie Mac’s <a href="https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Primary Mortgage Market Survey</a> for October 22, 2026, the average 30-year fixed rate was <strong>7.32%</strong>, down from 7.40% the week before but up from 6.19% a year earlier. The 15-year fixed averaged <strong>6.68%</strong>, down from 6.71% a week earlier and up from 5.44% a year ago. Freddie Mac said the decline offered "some relief" to prospective buyers.</p><p>What that means for refinancing: with averages more than a point above last year’s, refinancing mainly helps borrowers whose current rate is well above 7.32%, or who need to change their loan type or remove mortgage insurance. Anyone with a rate near or below today’s averages is unlikely to break even on a rate-and-term refinance. Freddie Mac publishes new averages every Thursday, so check the latest figure before you apply.</p>'
            . '<h2>How to calculate your refinance break-even point</h2><p>The basic formula is simple:</p><p><strong>Break-even (months) = total closing costs &divide; monthly savings</strong></p><p>If the new loan costs $6,000 to close and lowers your principal and interest payment by $200 a month, you break even after 30 months, or two and a half years. Every month you keep the loan after that is a net saving. The <a href="{tool:break-even-calculator}">break-even calculator</a> handles this math, and the <a href="{tool:refinance-calculator}">refinance calculator</a> adds the loan details.</p><p>Use the principal and interest payment for the comparison. Property taxes and homeowners insurance usually do not change with a refinance, so leaving them in can hide the real difference.</p>'
            . '<h2>How much does refinancing cost?</h2><p>According to <a href="https://myhome.freddiemac.com/refinancing/costs-of-refinancing" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Freddie Mac</a>, you can expect to spend 3% to 6% of your loan principal to refinance, with the total depending on your lender, credit score and location. The Federal Reserve’s <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/pubs/refinancings/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">consumer guide to mortgage refinancings</a> gives a similar range. Typical costs include:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Cost</th><th>What it pays for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Origination and underwriting fees</td><td>The lender’s cost of processing and approving the loan</td></tr><tr><td>Appraisal</td><td>An estimate of the home’s current value</td></tr><tr><td>Credit report</td><td>Pulling your credit history</td></tr><tr><td>Title services</td><td>Title search and lender’s title insurance</td></tr><tr><td>Government recording costs</td><td>Recording the new mortgage</td></tr><tr><td>Survey, attorney and tax service fees</td><td>Required in some states and by some lenders</td></tr><tr><td>Discount points (optional)</td><td>Prepaid interest to lower the rate; one point equals 1% of the loan</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-loan-estimate-en-1995/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Consumer Financial Protection Bureau</a> says a lender must give you a Loan Estimate within three business days of receiving your application. Collect estimates from several lenders on the same day and compare the closing cost totals line by line.</p><h3>Is a no-closing-cost refinance free?</h3><p>No. Freddie Mac says "there is no such thing as a free loan": lenders offering a no-cost refinance are probably charging a higher interest rate or rolling the costs into the loan balance. The CFPB describes the same trade-off as lender credits, which lower costs up front in exchange for a higher rate. That can still be a good deal if you expect to move or refinance again soon, because you pay less up front. Our guide to <a href="/mortgage-points-explained/">mortgage points</a> covers the opposite choice, paying more now for a lower rate.</p>'
            . '<h2>Should I refinance my home? A worked example</h2><p>Here is a hypothetical example. The new rate is set close to Freddie Mac’s October 22, 2026 average; it is an illustration, not a quote or forecast.</p><p>A homeowner borrowed $320,000 three years ago on a 30-year fixed loan at 8.25%. The principal and interest payment is <strong>$2,404.05</strong>, and after 36 payments the balance is about <strong>$311,700</strong> with 27 years left. A lender offers to refinance the $311,700 at 7.25%, with closing costs of <strong>$9,351</strong>, which is 3% of the loan and at the low end of Freddie Mac’s range.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Rate</th><th>Monthly P&amp;I</th><th>Monthly change</th><th>Break-even</th><th>Interest from here</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Keep current loan (27 years left)</td><td>8.25%</td><td>$2,404.05</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>about $467,200</td></tr><tr><td>New 30-year loan</td><td>7.25%</td><td>$2,126.34</td><td>&minus;$277.71</td><td>about 34 months</td><td>$453,784</td></tr><tr><td>New 27-year loan (same payoff date)</td><td>7.25%</td><td>$2,194.97</td><td>&minus;$209.08</td><td>about 45 months</td><td>$399,471</td></tr><tr><td>New 15-year loan</td><td>6.625%</td><td>$2,736.71</td><td>+$332.66</td><td>not a payment saving</td><td>$180,907</td></tr></tbody></table><p>What the numbers show:</p><ul><li><strong>The 30-year refinance</strong> cuts the payment by $277.71 a month and breaks even in about 34 months ($9,351 &divide; $277.71). But it adds three years of payments. Total interest from here falls only from about $467,200 to $453,784, and after the $9,351 in costs the lifetime saving is about $4,100.</li><li><strong>The 27-year option</strong> keeps the original payoff date. The monthly saving is smaller, so break-even takes about 45 months, but total interest drops by about $67,700, or about $58,400 after costs.</li><li><strong>The 15-year option</strong> raises the payment by $332.66 a month, so there is no break-even in the usual sense. In exchange it saves about $286,300 of interest compared with keeping the current loan, or about $277,000 after costs.</li></ul><p>If the homeowner rolled the $9,351 into a 30-year loan instead of paying cash, the payment would be about $2,190.13, a saving of $213.92 a month, and the balance would start higher. Try these scenarios with your own numbers in the <a href="{tool:refinance-calculator}">refinance calculator</a>, and see the payment schedule in the <a href="{tool:amortization-calculator}">amortization calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Should I refinance to a 15-year mortgage?</h2><p>A 15-year refinance makes sense when you can comfortably afford the higher payment and want to be debt-free sooner. Shorter loans often carry lower rates than 30-year loans, and you pay interest for half as long, so total interest falls sharply, as the example shows. The risks are a tighter monthly budget and less flexibility if income drops. A middle path is to refinance into a 30-year loan and voluntarily pay extra each month; you keep the option to pay less in a tough month, though you usually get a slightly higher rate than on a 15-year loan. Check whether the higher payment still leaves room for retirement saving with the <a href="{tool:401k-calculator}">401(k) calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>What about a cash-out refinance?</h2><p>A cash-out refinance replaces your loan with a larger one and pays you the difference in cash. People use it for renovations, to pay off higher-rate debt or for large expenses. Lenders limit how much of your equity you can borrow, and cash-out loans may carry a higher rate than a plain rate-and-term refinance. The key risk is that you turn unsecured debt into debt secured by your home, and you restart the clock on a larger balance. If you only need part of your equity and your current rate is low, a <a href="{tool:home-equity-loan-calculator}">home equity loan</a> may cost less overall than refinancing the whole mortgage at a higher rate.</p>'
            . '<h2>When should you not refinance?</h2><ul><li><strong>You will move before break-even.</strong> If you expect to sell in two years and break-even is three years, you lose money.</li><li><strong>Your current rate is already low.</strong> Many borrowers locked in rates well below today’s market; replacing them rarely pays.</li><li><strong>You are far into the loan.</strong> Late in a mortgage most of each payment goes to principal. Restarting a 30-year term moves you back to interest-heavy payments.</li><li><strong>Closing costs wipe out the saving.</strong> A rate cut that saves $60 a month will take years to recover $8,000 in fees.</li><li><strong>Your credit has slipped.</strong> A lower score can mean a higher offered rate.</li><li><strong>There is a prepayment penalty</strong> on your current loan. Check your note; the Loan Estimate also shows whether the new loan has one.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Does shopping for a refinance hurt your credit?</h2><p>Only a little, if you shop within a short window. According to the <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-exactly-happens-when-a-mortgage-lender-checks-my-credit-en-2005/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">CFPB</a>, multiple credit checks from mortgage lenders within a 45-day window are recorded on your credit report as a single inquiry, so the effect is the same whether you ask one lender or five, as long as the last check is within 45 days of the first. Shopping for a different type of loan, such as a car loan, at the same time counts separately. The CFPB adds that the small effect of an extra inquiry is usually outweighed by the savings from comparing offers. Plan to request all of your Loan Estimates within a week or two so the quotes are comparable and the credit impact stays minimal.</p>'
            . '<h2>How to refinance, step by step</h2><ol><li><strong>Know your numbers:</strong> current balance, rate, remaining term and payment.</li><li><strong>Check your credit</strong> and estimate your home’s value and equity.</li><li><strong>Request Loan Estimates</strong> from at least three lenders on the same day.</li><li><strong>Compare</strong> rate, points, lender credits and total closing costs, not just the payment.</li><li><strong>Calculate break-even and total interest</strong> for each offer.</li><li><strong>Lock your rate</strong> once you choose, and review the Closing Disclosure before signing.</li></ol>'
            . '<h2>Should I refinance my student loans or auto loan too?</h2><p>The same break-even logic applies to other debts, with different trade-offs. Refinancing federal student loans with a private lender can lower the rate, but you give up federal benefits such as income-driven repayment and forgiveness programs; see our guide to <a href="/how-to-pay-off-student-loans-faster-2026/">paying off student loans faster</a> and the <a href="{tool:student-loan-calculator}">student loan calculator</a>. For cars, fees are usually smaller but the loan is shorter and the car loses value; our guide <a href="/should-i-refinance-my-car/">Should I refinance my car?</a> and the <a href="{tool:auto-loan-calculator}">auto loan calculator</a> walk through it.</p>'
            . '<h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/mortgage-points-explained/">Mortgage points explained</a></li><li><a href="/how-much-house-can-i-afford/">How much house can I afford?</a></li><li><a href="/first-time-home-buyer-guide-2026/">First-time home buyer guide</a></li><li><a href="/should-i-refinance-my-car/">Should I refinance my car?</a></li><li><a href="{tool:mortgage-calculator}">Mortgage calculator</a></li></ul>'
            . '<p><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href="https://myhome.freddiemac.com/refinancing/costs-of-refinancing" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Freddie Mac, Understanding the costs of refinancing</a> (3% to 6% of principal, fee list, no-cost loans); <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-loan-estimate-en-1995/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">CFPB, What is a Loan Estimate?</a>; <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/how-should-i-use-lender-credits-and-points-also-called-discount-points-en-136/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">CFPB, lender credits and points</a>; <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-exactly-happens-when-a-mortgage-lender-checks-my-credit-en-2005/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">CFPB, mortgage credit checks</a>; <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/pubs/refinancings/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Federal Reserve, A Consumer’s Guide to Mortgage Refinancings</a>; <a href="https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Freddie Mac Primary Mortgage Market Survey</a> (October 22, 2026). Example payments computed with the standard amortization formula. Checked October 24, 2026.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal tax or financial advice.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'Should I refinance my mortgage if rates drop 1%?', 'Possibly. In our example, a one-point drop from 8.25% to 7.25% on a $311,700 balance saved $277.71 a month and broke even in about 34 months on $9,351 of costs. Whether it pays for you depends on your balance, closing costs and how long you keep the loan.' ],
            [ 'When should I refinance my home?', 'Refinance when the monthly saving recovers your closing costs well before you expect to sell or pay off the home, and when total interest over the period you will keep the loan is lower. Better credit, more equity or a switch from an adjustable to a fixed rate can also justify it.' ],
            [ 'When should I refinance, and how is break-even calculated?', 'Divide total closing costs by the monthly reduction in principal and interest. For example, $6,000 in costs and a $200 monthly saving give a 30-month break-even. If you will keep the loan longer than that, the refinance saves money on a monthly basis.' ],
            [ 'How much are closing costs on a refinance?', 'Freddie Mac says to expect 3% to 6% of the loan principal, depending on the lender, your credit score and location. On a $300,000 loan that is $9,000 to $18,000. Compare Loan Estimates from several lenders on the same day, because fees and rates vary.' ],
            [ 'Should I refinance to a 15 year mortgage?', 'If you can comfortably afford the higher payment, a 15-year refinance usually carries a lower rate and cuts total interest sharply. In our example it saved about $286,300 of interest versus the old loan but raised the payment by $333 a month. Keep an emergency fund first.' ],
            [ 'Should I refinance my student loans?', 'Refinancing private student loans to a lower rate can save money. Refinancing federal loans with a private lender ends access to federal options such as income-driven repayment, deferment and forgiveness programs, so weigh those benefits before switching. Compare offers with a student loan calculator.' ],
            [ 'Should I refinance my auto loan?', 'An auto refinance can pay off if your rate is well above current offers, your credit has improved and fees are low. Watch for a longer term that raises total interest while the car loses value. Our refinance-my-car guide covers the break-even math.' ],
            [ 'Is a no-closing-cost refinance a good idea?', 'It can be if you expect to move or refinance again within a few years, because you avoid up-front fees. Freddie Mac notes the costs do not disappear: the lender usually charges a higher rate or adds them to the balance, which costs more over a long period.' ],
        ],
    ],
    [
        'slug'       => 'year-end-tax-planning-checklist',
        'title'      => 'Year-End Tax Planning Checklist for 2026',
        'keyword'    => 'year end tax planning',
        'desc'       => 'A year-end tax planning checklist for 2026 with IRS-verified limits: 401(k), IRA, HSA, standard deduction, SALT cap, new charitable rules, RMDs and gifts.',
        'excerpt'    => 'A step-by-step year-end tax planning checklist for 2026, with every limit checked against IRS.gov.',
        'publish_at' => '2026-10-29 09:00',
        'answer'     => 'Year-end tax planning for 2026 means acting before December 31: max out 401(k) deferrals ($24,500), harvest investment losses, take required minimum distributions, time charitable gifts around the new 0.5% AGI floor and the $1,000/$2,000 non-itemizer deduction, use the $19,000 gift exclusion, and check withholding. IRA and HSA contributions can wait until April 15, 2027.',
        'takeaways'  => [
            'Most moves, including 401(k) deferrals, RMDs and loss harvesting, must happen by December 31, 2026.',
            'IRA and HSA contributions for 2026 can be made until the April 15, 2027 filing deadline.',
            'From 2026, non-itemizers can deduct up to $1,000 ($2,000 joint) of cash gifts to charity, and itemizers face a 0.5% of AGI floor.',
            'The 2026 SALT deduction limit is $40,400, reduced above $505,000 of MAGI but not below $10,000.',
        ],
        'content'    => '<p class="lead"><strong>Year-end tax planning</strong> is the review you do before December 31 to lower the tax you will owe for the year, while there is still time to act. Several rules change for tax year 2026 under the 2025 tax law, including a new charitable deduction for people who do not itemize, a floor on charitable deductions for those who do and a higher state and local tax cap. This checklist walks through each item with the 2026 limits confirmed on IRS.gov. To see how the moves affect your bill, use the <a href="{tool:income-tax-calculator}">income tax calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Year-end tax planning: 2026 limits at a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Item (tax year 2026)</th><th>Limit</th><th>Deadline</th><th>IRS source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>401(k), 403(b), 457, TSP employee deferral</td><td>$24,500</td><td>December 31, 2026 (through payroll)</td><td>IR-2025-111</td></tr><tr><td>Catch-up, age 50 and over</td><td>$8,000 (total $32,500)</td><td>December 31, 2026</td><td>IR-2025-111</td></tr><tr><td>Catch-up, ages 60 to 63</td><td>$11,250</td><td>December 31, 2026</td><td>IR-2025-111</td></tr><tr><td>IRA contribution (traditional + Roth)</td><td>$7,500, plus $1,100 at age 50+</td><td>April 15, 2027</td><td>IR-2025-111</td></tr><tr><td>HSA contribution</td><td>$4,400 self-only, $8,750 family, plus $1,000 at 55+</td><td>April 15, 2027</td><td>Rev. Proc. 2025-19</td></tr><tr><td>Standard deduction</td><td>$16,100 single/MFS, $32,200 joint, $24,150 head of household</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Rev. Proc. 2025-32</td></tr><tr><td>State and local tax (SALT) deduction limit</td><td>$40,400 ($20,200 MFS)</td><td>Taxes paid by December 31</td><td>Pub. 505 (2026)</td></tr><tr><td>Annual gift tax exclusion</td><td>$19,000 per recipient</td><td>December 31, 2026</td><td>Rev. Proc. 2025-32</td></tr><tr><td>Qualified charitable distribution (age 70&frac12;+)</td><td>$111,000</td><td>December 31, 2026</td><td>Notice 2025-67</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<h2>Latest update: what changed for 2026 and 2027</h2><ul><li><strong>New 2026 rules from the 2025 law.</strong> The One Big Beautiful Bill Act, which the IRS calls the Working Families Tax Cuts, brings three changes that matter for year-end planning in 2026: a charitable deduction of up to $1,000 ($2,000 joint) for people who do not itemize, a 0.5% of AGI floor on charitable deductions for those who do and a SALT cap of $40,400. Each is covered below.</li><li><strong>2027 HSA limits are out.</strong> IRS Revenue Procedure 2026-24 sets the 2027 HSA limits at $4,500 for self-only coverage and $9,000 for family coverage, up from $4,400 and $8,750 in 2026.</li><li><strong>2027 tax brackets and standard deduction: not yet released.</strong> As of October 24, 2026, the IRS newsroom had not published the tax year 2027 inflation adjustments. Last year’s version, Rev. Proc. 2025-32, appeared in the Internal Revenue Bulletin on November 3, 2025.</li><li><strong>2027 401(k) and IRA limits: expected in November.</strong> The IRS announced the 2026 limits on November 13, 2025 (IR-2025-111), and the 2027 limits had not been announced as of October 24, 2026.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>1. Have you maxed out your workplace retirement plan?</h2><p>According to IRS news release <a href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IR-2025-111</a> (November 13, 2025), the 2026 employee deferral limit for 401(k), 403(b), most 457 plans and the Thrift Savings Plan is $24,500. Workers 50 and older can add $8,000, for $32,500, and those aged 60 to 63 have a higher catch-up of $11,250. Deferrals must come out of a paycheck dated in 2026, so raise your percentage now if you want to close a gap with the last few paychecks.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> a single worker earning $95,000 has deferred $18,000 so far and has four paychecks left. Raising the deferral by $750 a paycheck adds $3,000 of pre-tax savings. In a 22% federal bracket that cuts this year’s federal income tax by about $660, before any state tax. Model the long-term effect with the <a href="{tool:401k-calculator}">401(k) calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>2. Can you still fund an IRA or HSA for 2026?</h2><p>Yes, and you have more time. IRA contributions for 2026 can be made up to the due date of your return, April 15, 2027, not counting extensions. The 2026 limit is $7,500, plus a $1,100 catch-up at age 50 or older (IR-2025-111). Deductibility of traditional IRA contributions and eligibility for Roth contributions depend on your income and workplace plan coverage. The <a href="{tool:ira-calculator}">IRA calculator</a> shows how contributions grow.</p><p>If you had a high-deductible health plan, the 2026 health savings account limits are $4,400 for self-only coverage and $8,750 for family coverage, plus $1,000 if you are 55 or older (Rev. Proc. 2025-19; IRS Publication 969). HSA contributions for 2026 can also be made until April 15, 2027.</p>'
            . '<h2>3. Should you harvest investment losses before December 31?</h2><p>If you sold investments at a gain this year, selling losing positions in a taxable account can offset them, and up to $3,000 of net loss ($1,500 married filing separately) can reduce ordinary income (<a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Publication 550</a>). The trade date must be December 31, 2026 or earlier, and you must avoid buying substantially identical securities 30 days before or after, including in an IRA. Our full guide to <a href="/tax-loss-harvesting/">tax loss harvesting</a> has a worked example. Check where your gains fall in the 2026 capital gains brackets, which start the 15% rate above $49,450 of taxable income for single filers and $98,900 for joint filers (Rev. Proc. 2025-32), with the <a href="{tool:capital-gains-tax-calculator}">capital gains tax calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>4. How do the 2026 charitable deduction changes affect year-end giving?</h2><p>The One Big Beautiful Bill Act, which the IRS now calls the Working Families Tax Cuts, changes charitable deductions starting with tax year 2026:</p><ul><li><strong>Non-itemizers get a deduction.</strong> According to the <a href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/one-big-beautiful-bill-provisions" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS</a>, people who take the standard deduction can deduct up to $1,000 of cash contributions to eligible charities, or $2,000 for married couples filing jointly. Gifts to donor-advised funds do not qualify, nor do noncash gifts such as clothing or stock, or carryovers from earlier years.</li><li><strong>Itemizers face a floor.</strong> <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p505" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Publication 505</a> says that beginning in 2026, itemizers can only deduct charitable contributions that exceed 0.5% of adjusted gross income.</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> a couple with $150,000 of AGI who itemize and give $5,000 to charity in 2026 face a floor of $750 (0.5% of $150,000), so $4,250 is deductible. A couple who take the standard deduction and give $2,500 in cash can deduct $2,000.</p><p>Because of the floor, some itemizers may consider "bunching", giving two years of donations in one year so they clear both the floor and the standard deduction. If you are 70&frac12; or older, a qualified charitable distribution from an IRA, up to $111,000 for 2026 under IRS Notice 2025-67, goes straight to charity and is excluded from income, which can be useful whether or not you itemize. Keep receipts or bank records for every gift.</p>'
            . '<h2>5. Will you itemize or take the standard deduction?</h2><p>For 2026, the standard deduction is $16,100 for single filers and married filing separately, $32,200 for married couples filing jointly and $24,150 for heads of household (Rev. Proc. 2025-32). Itemizing only helps if mortgage interest, state and local taxes, charitable gifts and other deductions add up to more.</p><h3>The 2026 SALT cap</h3><p>IRS Publication 505 (2026) sets the state and local tax deduction limit at $40,400 ($20,200 if married filing separately). The limit is reduced when modified adjusted gross income exceeds $505,000 ($252,500 married filing separately), but not below $10,000 ($5,000 married filing separately). If you are near the line between itemizing and not, the timing of a property tax payment or a charitable gift between December and January can move you to the better side for each year.</p><h3>The senior deduction</h3><p>Publication 505 also describes a new deduction of up to $6,000 for eligible people born before January 2, 1962 ($12,000 if both spouses qualify), limited when modified AGI exceeds $75,000 ($150,000 joint). It is available whether or not you itemize, which can make the standard deduction more attractive for older filers.</p>'
            . '<h2>6. Have you taken your required minimum distribution?</h2><p>The IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-required-minimum-distributions-rmds" rel="noopener" target="_blank">RMD page</a> says you generally must start withdrawals from IRAs and workplace plans at age 73. The first RMD can wait until April 1 of the following year, but every later RMD is due by December 31. Taking two in one year can push you into a higher bracket, so plan the first one carefully. Roth IRAs and designated Roth accounts in 401(k) and 403(b) plans have no RMDs while the owner is alive. Missing an RMD triggers a 25% excise tax on the shortfall, reduced to 10% if corrected within two years.</p>'
            . '<h2>7. Should you do a Roth conversion this year?</h2><p>A Roth conversion moves money from a traditional IRA to a Roth IRA. The converted amount is taxable income for the year of conversion, and the conversion must be completed by December 31 to count for 2026. It can make sense in a lower-income year, for example after retiring but before Social Security and RMDs begin. Watch the knock-on effects: higher income can affect the capital gains rate, the 3.8% net investment income tax thresholds of $200,000 single and $250,000 joint (<a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc559" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Topic 559</a>), the senior deduction phase-out and Medicare premiums later.</p>'
            . '<h2>8. Are you using the annual gift exclusion?</h2><p>For 2026, the first $19,000 of gifts to any one person is excluded from taxable gifts (Rev. Proc. 2025-32). A married couple can give $38,000 to each recipient by splitting gifts. Gifts above the exclusion generally require a gift tax return but usually no tax, because they count against the lifetime basic exclusion, which the 2025 law set at $15,000,000 for 2026. Unused annual exclusions do not carry over, so gifts must be completed by December 31. The <a href="{tool:estate-tax-calculator}">estate tax calculator</a> shows where large estates stand.</p>'
            . '<h2>9. Is your withholding or estimated tax on track?</h2><p>According to the <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/f1040es.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">2026 Form 1040-ES</a> instructions, you generally avoid an underpayment penalty if withholding and timely estimated payments cover at least 90% of your 2026 tax or 100% of the tax shown on your 2025 return; the second figure becomes 110% if your 2025 AGI was over $150,000 ($75,000 married filing separately). The fourth estimated payment for 2026 is due January 15, 2027. Withholding is treated as paid evenly through the year, so increasing it in December can fix a shortfall from earlier quarters. Self-employed? Estimate your bill with the <a href="{tool:self-employment-tax-calculator}">self-employment tax calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>10. Other year-end tax planning items</h2><ul><li><strong>Flexible spending accounts:</strong> check your plan’s deadline and any carryover or grace period so you do not forfeit money.</li><li><strong>Business owners:</strong> review the timing of equipment purchases, invoices and expenses with your tax professional.</li><li><strong>Education:</strong> paying spring tuition in December may affect which year an education credit applies to; check the rules for your situation.</li><li><strong>Records:</strong> collect receipts for charity, medical costs and property taxes now, while they are easy to find.</li><li><strong>Big picture:</strong> confirm your savings rate supports your goals with our <a href="/retirement-planning-how-much-need-2026/">retirement planning guide</a>.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Year-end tax planning checklist: what to do before December 31</h2><ol><li>Raise 401(k) deferrals to reach your 2026 limit.</li><li>Harvest losses in taxable accounts, avoiding wash sales.</li><li>Take RMDs and decide on any qualified charitable distribution.</li><li>Decide on a Roth conversion.</li><li>Time charitable gifts around the 0.5% floor or the non-itemizer deduction.</li><li>Pay deductible state and local taxes you plan to claim, within the SALT limit.</li><li>Complete annual exclusion gifts.</li><li>Adjust withholding if you are short.</li><li>Note the items that can wait: IRA and HSA contributions until April 15, 2027.</li></ol>'
            . '<h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/tax-loss-harvesting/">Tax loss harvesting: how it works, rules and examples</a></li><li><a href="/retirement-planning-how-much-need-2026/">How much you need to retire</a></li><li><a href="/index-fund-investing-beginners-guide-2026/">Index fund investing for beginners</a></li><li><a href="/should-i-refinance-my-mortgage/">Should I refinance my mortgage?</a></li></ul>'
            . '<p><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS IR-2025-111</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-32.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p505" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Publication 505 (2026)</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/one-big-beautiful-bill-provisions" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Working Families Tax Cuts provisions</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/n-25-67.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Notice 2025-67</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Publication 969</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-26-24.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Revenue Procedure 2026-24</a> (2027 HSA limits); <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-required-minimum-distributions-rmds" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS RMD rules</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/f1040es.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Form 1040-ES (2026)</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Publication 550</a>. All figures checked on IRS.gov on October 24, 2026.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal tax or financial advice.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'What is year end tax planning?', 'Year-end tax planning is reviewing your finances before December 31 to reduce the tax you owe for the year. Typical moves include increasing retirement contributions, harvesting investment losses, timing charitable gifts and deductions, taking required distributions and checking withholding while there is still time to change the result.' ],
            [ 'What is the 401(k) limit for 2026?', 'The IRS set the 2026 employee deferral limit at $24,500 for 401(k), 403(b), most 457 plans and the Thrift Savings Plan. Workers 50 and older can add an $8,000 catch-up, for $32,500, and those aged 60 to 63 can add $11,250 instead. Deferrals must be made through 2026 payroll.' ],
            [ 'What year-end tax planning moves can wait until April?', 'IRA and HSA contributions for tax year 2026 can be made until the April 15, 2027 filing deadline. The 2026 IRA limit is $7,500, plus $1,100 at age 50 and older. HSA limits are $4,400 for self-only and $8,750 for family coverage, plus $1,000 at 55.' ],
            [ 'Can I deduct charity if I take the standard deduction in 2026?', 'Yes. Starting in 2026, people who do not itemize can deduct up to $1,000 of cash gifts to eligible charities, or $2,000 for married couples filing jointly. Gifts to donor-advised funds, noncash gifts such as clothing or stock, and carryovers from earlier years do not qualify.' ],
            [ 'What is the 0.5% floor on charitable deductions?', 'Beginning in 2026, taxpayers who itemize can deduct charitable contributions only to the extent they exceed 0.5% of adjusted gross income. With $150,000 of AGI, the first $750 of gifts is not deductible, so a $5,000 donation produces a $4,250 deduction.' ],
            [ 'What is the SALT deduction limit for 2026?', 'IRS Publication 505 sets the 2026 state and local tax deduction limit at $40,400, or $20,200 for married filing separately. It is reduced when modified adjusted gross income exceeds $505,000, or $252,500 for married filing separately, but it cannot fall below $10,000, or $5,000.' ],
            [ 'What is the gift tax exclusion for 2026?', 'For 2026, you can give up to $19,000 to each person without it counting as a taxable gift, and a married couple can give $38,000 per recipient by splitting gifts. Larger gifts usually require a gift tax return but count against the $15,000,000 lifetime basic exclusion.' ],
            [ 'When is the RMD deadline for 2026?', 'Required minimum distributions for 2026 are due by December 31, 2026. The only exception is your first RMD, which can be delayed until April 1 of the year after you turn 73. Missing an RMD triggers a 25% excise tax, reduced to 10% if corrected within two years.' ],
        ],
    ],
    ];
}
